from ./Principles import Principles
from ./RunningExample import RunningExample
from ./structure import StructuralContext
from ./text import Text
from ./meaning import Meaning
from ./model import SemanticModel
from ./measures import Measures
from ./instructions import Instructions
from ./LanguageData import LanguageData
from ./Determinism import Determinism
from ./Configuration import Configuration
from ./Output import Output
from ../cli/Analyze import Analyze

from ./meaning/Facts export Fact, FactForm
from ./meaning/Conditions export Condition, ConditionKind
from ./meaning/Provenance export SourceRecord
from ./meaning/DerivationStates export DerivationState
from ./meaning/Strengths export Strength
from ./meaning/Relationships export RelationshipKind
from ./model/OpenItems export OpenItem, OpenItemKind
from ./measures export Measure
from ./Configuration export AnalysisConfig
from ./measures/Expectations export Expectation
from ./measures/Readiness export ReadinessThreshold
from ./meaning/Patterns export StatementPattern
from ./meaning/Normalization export EquivalenceRule
from ./LanguageData export
    ValueClass,
    WordList,
    WordMap

export anchor Analysis:
    description:
        A Piton specification says things in two ways. Some of it is
        structure: anchors, properties, references, values, which the
        compiler reads exactly. Most of it is prose, which can be precise or
        vague, complete or full of holes, and which the compiler does not
        read at all.

        A specification can therefore be perfectly valid Piton and still say
        almost nothing. An anchor with a name and a few empty properties
        compiles cleanly. Analysis is how the difference becomes visible: it
        works out what a specification actually says, what follows from it,
        and what it leaves open, without guessing and without asking a
        language model.

    theStory:
        This section reads like a pipeline, and each chapter is one stage of
        it.

        ```text
        Piton already gives us structure.                     structure
            |
        Some of it contains prose.
        Break the prose into statements.                      text
        Find the words, and what kind of word each is.
        Find what each statement is about, and what it says.
            |
        Link those names to things the specification defines. meaning
        Normalize wordings we have rules for.
        Turn statements into facts, with their conditions.
        Derive what follows from them.
        Keep everything else as unresolved.
            |
        Connect the facts into one model.                     model
        Find its gaps, ambiguities and contradictions.
            |
        Measure how explicit, complete and connected it is,   measures
        and how much an implementer would have to invent.
            |
        Compile what is known into explicit instructions      instructions
        for an implementation agent.
        ```

        The stages are described in @{StructuralContext}, @{Text},
        @{Meaning}, @{SemanticModel}, @{Measures} and @{Instructions}, in that
        order. Every stage is illustrated with the same small specification,
        @{RunningExample}, which is the best place to start.

    principles: @{Principles}

    alongTheWay:
        Three sections support every stage. @{LanguageData} lists everything
        analysis knows about words. @{Determinism} explains what makes the
        same input always give the same result, and where that guarantee
        stops. @{Configuration} lists what a project can change.

    using:
        Analysis is run with @{Analyze}, and what it shows is described in
        @{Output}.

    whatIsPublic:
        The shapes of analysis results, Fact, Condition, SourceRecord and
        OpenItem, are exported, along with the kinds they are built from, so
        that other tools and specifications can refer to them. So are the
        anchors a project uses to configure analysis: AnalysisConfig,
        Expectation, ReadinessThreshold, StatementPattern, EquivalenceRule,
        WordList, WordMap and ValueClass. Everything else, including every
        built-in rule and word list, is internal to this section.
